@startuml architecture-packages
' The layers of the backend, as the packages they live in.
'
!include _style.puml
left to right direction

package "Presentation" {
  node "frontend: screens, components,\nAPI client\nscripts: command-line tools\nscheduler: cron entry point" as P
}

package "Interface" {
  node "src/api: routes, middleware,\nupload validation, scope\nsrc/auth: user manager,\nsessions, CSRF\nsrc/graph: job workflow,\napplication workflow, nodes\nsrc/evaluation: metrics,\nrunner, security probes" as I
}

package "Domain" {
  node "src/jobs: board adapters,\nHTTP client, dedup,\nspam screen\nsrc/matching: six scorers,\nweights\nsrc/resume: parser, tailoring,\nATS, renderer\nsrc/llm: providers, cache,\nresilience\nsrc/research: grounded insights" as D
}

package "Infrastructure" {
  node "src/security: allowlist,\npermissions, fences,\nlimits, audit\nsrc/db: models, scoped\naccessors\nsrc/storage: per-user layout\nsrc/rag: Chroma vector store\nsrc/core: logging, text helpers" as F
}

P -[hidden]right-> I
D -[hidden]right-> F
P -[hidden]down-> D

P --> I : depends on
D --> F : depends on

note bottom of I
  The dependency rule runs from the layer beside a module to the one
  below it: a domain module may call another domain module and the
  infrastructure, but it does not import from the interface layer and it
  does not know that HTTP exists.
end note

@enduml
